{"connection_id": "BC-6ecddea6766ccd3e72de", "ref": "Augustine, City of God 19.15", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "creation, animal rule, slavery after sin", "note": "Augustine argues that the created order gives humans rule over irrational creatures, while human slavery is bound to sin; this Christian reading separates animal dominion from a natural right to own persons."}
{"connection_id": "BC-38907a1b1650c9abce52", "ref": "Augustine, Confessions 1.1.1", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Human creation directed toward God", "note": "The commentary treats being created as the ground of belonging to God; Augustine opens by interpreting human creation as oriented toward God and rest in God, adding an account of the human longing that accompanies creaturely dependence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f8d95fd9ea607d8af973", "ref": "Didache 4:10", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "master and slave hope in the same God", "note": "The Didache forbids bitter commands to male or female slaves because both slave and master hope in the same God; it applies common divine lordship to household conduct."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6458cf9bb2801c7cc78b", "ref": "Gregory of Nyssa, Homilies on Ecclesiastes 4", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "buying humans made in God's image", "note": "Gregory's homily on Ecclesiastes 2:7 attacks the purchase of human beings by appealing to their divine image and created freedom, a pointed patristic challenge to calling a person property."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c334e7ef6d3847c7f101", "ref": "Ignatius, To Polycarp 4:3", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "slaves serve for God's glory and better freedom", "note": "Ignatius tells enslaved Christians to serve more diligently for God's glory so that they may receive a better freedom from God, preserving the tension between social servitude and divine freedom."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d7a6542ba9d5a47d68c4", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.6.19", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Believers are not their own", "note": "The commentary says the human servant belongs to an owner; Paul tells the Corinthians they are not their own because they were bought at a price, giving a direct ownership formulation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dd26af35e48a52c4c027", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.6.20", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bought with a price and glorify God", "note": "Believers are said to have been bought at a price and therefore to glorify God bodily, joining ownership language to a lived act of worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ca87099b0e81f5f85c28", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.7.22", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A free person called in Christ is Christ's slave", "note": "The commentary says free and enslaved people alike are God's servants; Paul calls the free person who is called by the Lord Christ's slave, making freedom and divine belonging coexist."}
{"connection_id": "BC-56c0e1d60b321b5cdfdb", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.7.22", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "slave as Lord's freedperson, free as Christ's slave", "note": "Paul reverses ordinary social labels: the enslaved called in the Lord is free, while the free person is Christ's slave; it sharply reframes the commentary's fixed owner-slave status."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7a0bf288d067728348c4", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.7.23", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Bought with a price, not slaves of humans", "note": "The commentary places divine ownership above the human master's claim; Paul says believers were bought with a price and must not become slaves of humans, explicitly limiting human ownership."}
{"connection_id": "BC-55e7df3dc6ba0ca4c8ab", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.7.23", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "bought at a price; do not become human slaves", "note": "Because believers were bought at a price, Paul warns them not to become slaves of humans; divine purchase becomes an argument against human mastery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2ab5332a13e5140bb029", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.9.19", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A free speaker makes himself a slave to others", "note": "The commentary says the servant's status is not initiated by choice because it follows creation; Paul says that though free he made himself a slave to all, offering a contrasting voluntary form of service for others."}
{"connection_id": "BC-29be2ab7b43b18be1d20", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.16", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Free people remain God's servants", "note": "The commentary says human freedom does not cancel being God's servant; Peter tells believers to live as free people while remaining God's servants, holding both statuses together."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3ef7b2e652b1180ca8b3", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.16", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "free people as slaves of God", "note": "Believers are to live as free people while also as God's slaves; the deliberate pairing gives a Christian reversal of the commentary's ordinary contrast between freedom and owned status."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f77696b89c7b9b4f63ba", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.1", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Slaves owe honor to human masters", "note": "The commentary's B006 gloss describes someone honored as if worshipped; Timothy tells enslaved believers to regard their masters as worthy of honor, adding a household rule that distinguishes human honor from divine worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3ac22fa9dc0a248cb55b", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.15", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "only Sovereign, King of kings, Lord of lords", "note": "The doxology calls God the only Sovereign and the King of kings and Lord of lords, a compact New Testament royal-master formulation beside Mālik."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4317c652e9b1345a33e9", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.5.10", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Everyone appears before Christ's judgment seat", "note": "The commentary joins malik with the Day of Judgment; Paul says all must appear before Christ's judgment seat and receive what is due, adding personal accountability to the sovereign judgment motif."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2c26a0410fedac2919d5", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.5.10", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all appear before Christ's judgment seat", "note": "All people must appear before Christ's judgment seat to receive according to their deeds, an individual accounting scene for the Day of dīn dimension."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0ba1962b7931a9817154", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.10.25", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Cornelius falls at a human's feet", "note": "Cornelius falls at Peter's feet and prostrates before him, staging the very transfer of honor to a human figure that the commentary's limit on human lords excludes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c35a2e6f06834c7ca7ae", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.10.26", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Peter refuses human prostration", "note": "Peter raises Cornelius and says that he too is human, turning the preceding act into an explicit rejection of worshipful elevation of a person."}
{"connection_id": "BC-beb0083bb12acc8af2a3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.14.15", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Human beings refuse worship and point to the Creator", "note": "The commentary calls God the one worshipped and treats humans as created servants; Paul and Barnabas reject a crowd's worship, identify themselves as human, and direct it to the living Creator, clarifying why a human must not take divine honor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d540a1a8151b0462d44d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.17.24", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God made the world and is Lord of heaven and earth", "note": "Paul calls the creator Lord of heaven and earth, a New Testament statement of comprehensive divine sovereignty over the worlds named in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-30bc9e73738832416e35", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.17.25", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The Creator gives life and is not served from need", "note": "The commentary grounds human service in being created and treats God as owner; Paul says the world's maker is not served by human hands as though needy, because he gives life and all things, distinguishing worship from supplying a master's lack."}
{"connection_id": "BC-27d97531a863a21b2303", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.17.25", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God is not served as needing anything", "note": "God is not served by human hands as though he needed anything, because he gives life and breath to all; it closely supports the commentary's insistence that the true owner lacks no helper and is not reduced by need."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0cd293de689270c8be0e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.17.31", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a fixed day for judging the world", "note": "Paul says God has fixed a day on which he will judge the world in righteousness, making the day-and-judgment element explicit in the same speech that calls God creator and Lord."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6b885e462bb9f17fa1de", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.4.24", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "direct prayer to creator as Sovereign Master", "note": "The community addresses God as despota, the Sovereign Master who made heaven, earth, sea, and all in them; it joins direct address, mastery, and creation in one prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b25f892bc814299c9ca0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.3.24", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "slaves serve the Lord Christ", "note": "In an instruction to household slaves, Paul says that the service ultimately rendered is to the Lord Christ and promises an inheritance, placing earthly service under a different master and reward."}
{"connection_id": "BC-85d111ecbcd2583c596d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.4.1", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Earthly masters have a Master in heaven", "note": "The commentary links a human owner to the higher owner who rules all; Paul tells masters to act justly because they too have a Master in heaven, limiting household power under divine authority."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2168821f8a50f4f4c909", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.6.9", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "earthly masters share a Master in heaven", "note": "Christian masters must stop threatening because their own and their slaves' Master is in heaven and shows no partiality; the verse limits human ownership from above."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9697586a7c42c63c207d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.4.7", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "no longer slave but child and heir", "note": "The believer is no longer a slave but a child and heir through God, changing the household relation from permanent servitude to filial inheritance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7114cd17d833cbb21bac", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.13", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "make straight paths for feet", "note": "Hebrews commands straight paths for the feet so that the lame may be healed rather than put out of joint, turning the made-level road into a communal moral action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d225d48ea94ff2711c3b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.28", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "acceptable worship with reverence and awe", "note": "The community is to offer acceptable worship to God with reverence and awe because it receives an unshakable kingdom, a liturgical form of humble service before sovereignty."}
{"connection_id": "BC-753eaae6e2a75b38c800", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.3.5", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moses faithful as a servant in God's house", "note": "The commentary pictures a human servant in the owner's house; Hebrews calls Moses faithful as a servant within God's house, placing the Mosaic figure in a divine household and clarifying the servant's subordinate role."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0be495fe86dfc34a5705", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.3.5", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses as servant in God's whole house", "note": "Moses is faithful as a servant in all God's house, preserving the commentary's house/master/servant relation in an explicitly theological household."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9766966eca68c5c19279", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.3.6", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The Son stands over the house", "note": "The commentary distinguishes the owner from the servant who belongs in the owner's house; Hebrews contrasts Moses the servant with Christ as Son over God's house, making household status the basis of a theological distinction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-321ac22195021b0edd2f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.3.6", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Christ as Son over God's house", "note": "The contrast makes Christ Son over God's house and the believers that house, distinguishing sonship and household membership from slave status."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cc597f24f672d3a923af", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.3.7", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all animal kinds are tamed", "note": "James says every kind of beast, bird, reptile, and sea creature is tamed by humanity, a precise New Testament counterpart to the commentary's language of animal domestication."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9cb41e7ddd8cce9de7d7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.3.8", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the tongue cannot be tamed", "note": "The immediately following contrast says no human can tame the tongue; it gives the tamed-animal figure a moral reversal in which the human speaker remains resistant to mastery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a5981b8d4dc8625bc49c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.4.12", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "One lawgiver and judge can save or destroy", "note": "The commentary says God alone is the owner and judge while humans must not make one another lords; James names one lawgiver and judge able to save and destroy, explicitly grounding the limit on judging others in God's singular authority."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5fdc478e582b22955ec9", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.1.38", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Aramaic/Hebrew rabbî as a teacher's title", "note": "The commentary's source label ر ب ب explains rabb as an obeyed master and human household owner; John's disciples address Jesus as Rabbi and the Gospel glosses the title as Teacher, showing the Semitic cognate in a teacher-authority setting."}

Script completed
