{"connection_id": "BC-afc5eaeaece2c7b91f3d", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.13.13", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "“Teacher and Lord”", "note": "Jesus accepts the disciples' titles “Teacher” and “Lord,” giving the human speech of lordship in the commentary an explicit Gospel parallel before the following reversal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d86cd6194af7f1d447c0", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.13.14", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The Lord and Teacher washes his disciples' feet", "note": "The commentary distinguishes the owner from the humiliated servant; Jesus explicitly takes the role of teacher and Lord while washing his disciples' feet, making a servant's lowly work the action of the master."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8f03402b480f837a8271", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.13.14", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Lord washes servants' feet", "note": "The one called Lord and Teacher washes disciples' feet and makes it exemplary; the action directly counters the commentary's asymmetry in which humiliation belongs only to the servant."}
{"connection_id": "BC-90ebfbc455cebc9a1888", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.13.16", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A servant is not greater than the master", "note": "The commentary's image depends on the servant's subordination to the owner; Jesus uses the servant/master relation to set the disciples' status after washing their feet, while the scene has already shown the master serving."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0182d24c560a88c1d763", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.15.15", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Disciples no longer called slaves but friends", "note": "The commentary presents the human as God's owned servant by creation; Jesus says his disciples are no longer slaves who lack knowledge of the master's affairs but friends who have been told what he heard, adding a Christian shift from subordination to disclosed intimacy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-88380de7f1c9a7e7ffdd", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.15.15", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "no longer servants but friends", "note": "Jesus no longer calls disciples servants because a servant does not know the master's affairs, but friends; intimate disclosure revises the distance assumed by the master-property model."}
{"connection_id": "BC-116a5d51aa63cdb9145d", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.8.35", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "slave and son in the household", "note": "Jesus says a slave does not remain in the household forever but the son does; it uses the house image to contrast slave impermanence with filial permanence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a2f7c47b366efdf09072", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.37", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "master serves watchful servants", "note": "The returning master dresses for service, seats his servants, and serves them; this deliberately reverses the commentary's one-way household hierarchy and its claim that the master does not lower himself."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4da3649d735d80a39225", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.19", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "returning son asks to be a hired servant", "note": "The returning son proposes admission to his father's house as a hired servant; paired with the father's response in verse 22, it makes the commentary's servant-at-the-master's-house image a precise Gospel encounter."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a13742fa53a31f8cbcbf", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.22", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "father restores the returning son with honor", "note": "The father answers the returning son's household shame with robe, ring, and sandals, making the master's house a place of restorative honor rather than the fixed abasement expected in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ff8feecdec01428d96e8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.17.10", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Servants say they have done their duty", "note": "The commentary's speaker says in the first-person plural that they worship and explains worship as obedient submission; Jesus has servants conclude that they have only done their duty, giving a concrete household form for service without claiming merit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d9bbf0b9081774a6bddd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.17.10", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "servants have done only their duty", "note": "After doing all commanded, disciples are to call themselves servants who have only done their duty; it supplies a Christian idiom of obedience and lowly self-description."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2ccaa510c81ff7eb5119", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.17.7", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "servant returns from plowing or shepherding", "note": "Jesus' household example assumes a servant who comes in from field work and still prepares the master's meal, preserving the literal labor-and-master scene that underlies the commentary's slave image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a42495b2792f5139f6d1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.3.5", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "leveling and smoothing the way", "note": "Luke's Isaian quotation retains valleys filled, mountains lowered, and rough places made smooth; it carries the commentary's rendered-road image into the Gospel's preparation for God's coming."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ebbbd0b26096be473042", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.6.46", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "calling Lord requires obedience", "note": "Jesus asks why people call him “Lord, Lord” but do not do what he says; the verse explicitly joins the master's title to obedience, not to verbal address alone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-87d1c88e3211955b4949", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.1.3", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prepare the Lord's way, make paths straight", "note": "Mark applies the wilderness-road summons to John's proclamation, making the straightened path a concrete Gospel preparation for the Lord rather than a bare travel image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7e4e37dc4b6ec81df58c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.10.42", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Gentile rulers lord it over others", "note": "Jesus names dominating rule as the practice of Gentile rulers, making human lordship a social pattern that his teaching will explicitly reject."}
{"connection_id": "BC-017466197bb07e1a1e86", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.10.43", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "greatness becomes service", "note": "The next verse reverses status: whoever wants greatness must become the others' servant, changing the master-slave scale from ownership to service."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a287a44856f294218608", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.10.45", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Son of Man serves and gives ransom", "note": "The Son of Man comes not to be served but to serve and give his life as a ransom, a concentrated counter-scene to a master who only receives service."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7e7d01d918d56e92af2e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.23", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A king settles accounts with servants", "note": "The commentary joins Malik and Dīn as sovereign and judgment; Jesus begins a parable with a king settling accounts with his servants, giving a household scene in which ownership and reckoning meet."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ef202cc5e1a26bfea939", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.25", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A debtor and his household sold into slavery", "note": "The commentary links Dīn to subduing, enslavement, and possession; in the king's reckoning a debtor is ordered sold with his family and possessions, concretely combining debt judgment with human ownership."}
{"connection_id": "BC-87ccb947fa92c3366bca", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.26", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "servant falls before master over debt", "note": "The servant falls down before his master and begs for patience over the debt, giving a literal scene of servile prostration, ownership, and reckoning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3f1b1646ab56386c8835", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.27", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "master releases servant and forgives debt", "note": "The master is moved with compassion, releases the servant, and cancels the debt; this masterly authority is expressed through liberation rather than permanent subjection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e103b3d83fab5db53c65", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.20.27", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Greatness as becoming a slave", "note": "The commentary calls worship the servant's utmost humbling and warns against humans making one another lords; Jesus says the first must become the others' slave, reversing status within a human hierarchy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a1dbf8ef7d80278b18b6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.20.28", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The Son of Man serves rather than receives service", "note": "The commentary presents the master as owner and the servant as the one humbled; Jesus describes the Son of Man as serving and giving his life, placing service in the role of the exalted figure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4ee2cd9ad140f9a34125", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.21.5", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "gentle king mounted on donkey", "note": "Matthew presents the king as gentle and mounted on a donkey, carrying Zechariah's master-humility-and-domesticated-animal figure into the Gospel entry scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7055ce9ffe071d757b69", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.23.8", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Rabbi title and shared brotherhood", "note": "The commentary warns against taking one another as lords; Jesus tells disciples not to seek the title Rabbi because they are brothers and have one teacher, a specific Christian limit on human religious authority."}
