{"connection_id": "BC-98ffa6c4792940a57bb9", "ref": "WLC:Ps.116.16", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "direct “I am your servant”; bonds loosed", "note": "The psalmist directly calls himself God's servant and son of God's maidservant, then says God has loosed his bonds; it keeps divine servanthood and liberation together in one prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5894d0fd39093cf61dc0", "ref": "WLC:Ps.119.91", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "All creation as God's servants", "note": "The commentary says people are God's servants by being brought into existence; the psalm says the created order stands by God's ordinances and calls all things his servants, extending the service image beyond humanity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-49469788d1fc591a8bdb", "ref": "WLC:Ps.123.2", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "servants' eyes to master's hand", "note": "The psalm compares Israel's eyes toward God to servants' eyes toward a master's hand and a maid's toward her mistress; it is an exact prayer-image of dependent, direct address."}
{"connection_id": "BC-198b0fdc3e4b36279fde", "ref": "WLC:Ps.123.3", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Servants watch their masters' hands", "note": "The commentary describes the owned servant's posture before a master; the psalm compares worshippers' gaze toward God to the eyes of male and female servants fixed on their masters' hands, giving a precise domestic image for dependence in prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c1e72690b6a20c4808aa", "ref": "WLC:Ps.23.4", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "third-person shepherd narration turns to “you”", "note": "Psalm 23 moves from “he” as the shepherd who leads in verses 1-3 to direct “you” in verse 4; its exact pronoun turn offers a literary analogue to the commentary's movement from third-person praise to second-person address, though its figure is shepherd and flock."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0aad3d67d4343aa91566", "ref": "WLC:Ps.24.1", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The earth and its fullness belong to the Lord", "note": "The commentary's malik image includes everything held in the owner's hand; this psalm assigns the earth and all within it to the Lord, extending the property image from household goods to the world."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b865caf40f2c3b8a282b", "ref": "WLC:Ps.24.1", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "earth and inhabitants belong to YHWH", "note": "The psalm's opening ownership formula gives YHWH the earth, its fullness, the world, and its inhabitants, an explicit scriptural horizon for “Lord of the worlds.”"}
{"connection_id": "BC-5813e4b84e3b90165c34", "ref": "WLC:Ps.32.10", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Bit and bridle restrain horse and mule", "note": "The commentary's B005 gloss makes a subdued camel an image of submission; this proverb says an uncomprehending person must be restrained like a horse or mule with bit and bridle, while using the image as a warning against stubbornness rather than praising domination."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8206b83218ae4e9162dd", "ref": "WLC:Ps.50.10", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cattle belong to God", "note": "God claims every forest animal and the cattle on a thousand hills; human possession of livestock is set within the creator's prior ownership."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0d7e330a2153b5979dfe", "ref": "WLC:Ps.8.8", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Humans given rule over livestock", "note": "The commentary opens with an owner of animals and then says humans themselves are God's servants by creation; the psalm places sheep, cattle, and wild animals under human rule, showing delegated animal mastery beneath divine creation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-303c569a1085bc08b316", "ref": "WLC:Ps.95.6", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Bowing and kneeling before the maker", "note": "The commentary calls worship the utmost humbling of the servant; the psalm invites worshippers to bow and kneel before their maker, giving bodily posture to its account of submission."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7172ce810602d54deda1", "ref": "WLC:Ps.95.6", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bow, kneel, maker", "note": "The congregation is summoned to bow down and kneel before YHWH its maker, a close liturgical image of embodied abasement before the creator."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7d726c11289345bf73e9", "ref": "WLC:Ps.95.7", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "People as sheep of God's hand", "note": "The commentary makes the servant God's owned person and places him in the master's house; the psalm calls the worshippers God's people and the sheep of his hand, combining belonging with a shepherd's care."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0f0cc6f8ca5e1aaf027a", "ref": "WLC:Ps.95.7", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "people of pasture, flock of God's hand", "note": "The worshippers are God's people and the flock of his hand; it combines animal dependence, the owner's hand, and collective divine belonging."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b84509352e08dbdc4b4c", "ref": "WLC:Zech.14.9", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew מלך for kingship", "note": "The commentary labels Arabic م ل ك for malik as owner and sovereign; Zechariah uses the cognate Hebrew מלך for the Lord's kingship over the whole earth, showing the sovereign sense without importing the commentary's separate property sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-56d9f6033e261c8ed1e5", "ref": "WLC:Zech.9.9", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "humble king riding a donkey", "note": "Zion's king comes righteous and victorious yet humble, mounted on a donkey; the royal figure unites authority, lowliness, and a domesticated animal in a configuration that reverses the commentary's unlowered master."}
{"connection_id": "BC-42a2984e5bb84b02e665", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 16:24", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Creation serves its maker", "note": "The commentary says all humans come to God as servants because he created them; Wisdom says creation itself serves its maker, extending the creator-servant relation from humanity to the natural order."}
{"connection_id": "BC-136f54baabac8051186c", "ref": "b. Qiddushin 20a", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "acquiring a Hebrew servant acquires a master", "note": "The Talmud's maxim that one who acquires a Hebrew servant acquires a master develops Torah's service law into a reversal: the owner must meet the servant's needs so fully that mastery is ethically displaced."}
{"connection_id": "BC-755f3b986ee54bc049bc", "ref": "b. Qiddushin 22b", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "ear piercing and servants of God", "note": "The Talmud explains the permanently pierced Hebrew servant through the Sinai claim that Israel belongs to God, criticizing one who acquires a human master; it is a direct Jewish limit on human lordship."}

